the site of the moon clips in the Sun  has fascinated generations of onlookers  our ancestors came up with a variety of  explanation some more accurate than  others but over the century it turned  out there was a lot more to learn from  solar eclipses than just how they worked  the spectacle of a total solar eclipse  has led scientists to new discoveries  about the Sun the solar system and the  physics of the universe itself when  humans first started studying the  heavens we didn't know much about what  our solar system really looked like but  solar eclipses did give us some hints  for example that the Sun must be further  away from us than the moon  ancient Greek astronomer Aristarchus  wanted to know how much further and  decided to figure it out with simple  geometry Aristarchus deduced but when  the moon is exactly half lit the  positions of the Sun Moon and Earth must  form a right angle triangle so he  measured the angle between the Sun and  the half moon and used that to calculate  the relative distances to the two  objects since the angle is large the Sun  must be many times further away from us  than the moon is and since they appear  to be the same size in the sky as we can  see during an eclipse the Sun must also  be many times bigger than the moon  Aristarchus was even one of the first to  suggest that the Earth orbits the Sun  and not the other way around though no  one took that idea very seriously fast  forward about 2,000 years and Victorian  scientists were taking solar eclipses  very seriously indeed many intellectuals  of that time were avid eclipse chasers  hoping to learn more about the Sun and  the strange halo of light visible around  it during an eclipse  the founding editor of nature wrote  about the strange halo named the corona  in the first edition of the journal  speculating on what it might be further  study of eclipses proves that the corona  was part of the Sun and not coming from  the moon as some had suggested we now  know that the corona is made from  extremely hot plasma shaped by the sun's  magnetic field it's only visible for the  naked eye  when the moon blocks out the much  brighter light from the Sun itself  around the same time as they were  studying the corona scientists spotted  an unknown line in a spectrum of  sunlight during an eclipse this line  turned out to be a new element helium  now known to be the second most common  element in both the Sun and in the  universe itself by 1919 a lot more was  known about the Sun so in May of that  year Arthur Eddington used a total  eclipse to study something completely  different how gravity bends light he  photographed a cluster of stars visible  near the edge of the eclipsed Sun but  the Stars looked like they were in a  different position than usual this was  because the light from the distant stars  had to pass so close to our Sun that the  sun's mass actually bent to the light an  effect called gravitational lensing it  was the first experimental proof of  Albert Einstein's general theory of  relativity it made Einstein an overnight  celebrity  in the almost 100 years since people  have continued to study the Sun the  solar system and the theory of general  relativity today most of this research  no longer requires waiting for a total  solar eclipse scientific instruments on  earth and in space can study the  universe in amazing detail but some  scientists still travel to the site of  total eclipses and even chase the sun's  shadow in superfast jet  just to see what further secrets these  spectacular events might reveal  [Music]
 

